.TH AVR\-VM\-VNC 1 "" "avr 0.1.6" "Avrea CLI"
.SH NAME
avr-vm-vnc \- Open a VNC desktop on a macOS VM (Screen Sharing) over an SSH tunnel.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avr vm vnc
[OPTIONS] VM_ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
Open a VNC desktop on a macOS VM (Screen Sharing) over an SSH tunnel.
.PP
Forwards a local port to the guest's Screen Sharing service (:5900) through the VM's SSH endpoint, so the desktop is never exposed publicly. Holds the tunnel open until Ctrl-C; pass --launch to also open Screen Sharing.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIVM_ID\fR
\&
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-org\fR \fITEXT\fR
Organization ID. Uses default org if not specified (see: avr config set org).
.TP
\fB\-\-local\-port\fR \fIINTEGER RANGE\fR
Local port to bind (default: an unused port).
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity\fR \fIPATH\fR
Private key file to pass to ssh as -i.
.TP
\fB\-\-launch\fR / \fB\-\-no\-launch\fR
Also start a local VNC client (macOS Screen Sharing), instead of just printing the connect command.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\fR
Print the tunnel and client commands and exit, without opening the tunnel.
